# omarabedelkader.github.io

This repository builds and publishes a personal academic website and CVs.

## What is happening here?

The project uses **Markdown sources** plus a small **Python + Pandoc build pipeline**:

- `sources/` contains the content (`site.md`, `cv.md`, `cv-fr.md`, and blog posts under `sources/blog/`).
- `build/` contains Python scripts that convert Markdown into:
  - `docs/index.html` and `docs/fr/index.html` (website pages for GitHub Pages)
  - `docs/blog/`, `docs/fr/blog/`, and RSS feeds generated from Markdown blog posts
  - `cv-full/` (full CV) and `cv-short/` (one-page CV), each as `.md` and `.pdf`, in English and French
- `templates/` contains the website static assets (`style.css`, `main.js`, `header.html`, favicon).
- `resources/` contains citation and LaTeX support files (`publications.bib`, `apa.csl`, `header.tex`).
- `runner/` contains shell wrappers that set up a virtual environment, install dependencies, and run each build script.

## Build flow

`run.sh` creates/activates `.venv`, installs `requirements.txt`, then runs seven tasks. It does everything except commit and push:

1. Check Google Scholar and conf.researchr.org and import what is new (see "Monthly automation")
2. Translate `sources/cv.md` to `sources/cv-fr.md`
3. Build the full CV in `cv-full/`, then the short CV in `cv-short/`
4. Translate `sources/site.md` to `sources/site-fr.md`
5. Translate published blog posts from `sources/blog/*.md` to `sources/blog-fr/*.md`
6. Add news lines for items that are new in the sources
7. Build `docs/index.html`, `docs/fr/index.html`, one page per section, the short CV page, blog pages, RSS feeds, and `docs/sitemap.xml`

Task 1 needs the network; if a source cannot be reached it prints a warning and the build continues. `SKIP_SYNC=1 bash run.sh` skips it.

The translator protects Markdown links, URLs, inline code, fenced code blocks, frontmatter, raw LaTeX commands, and the `{{PUBLICATIONS_FROM_BIB}}` token. The website build copies CSS/JS/favicon into `docs/`, converts the site Markdown to HTML, copies CV PDFs into `docs/cv/`, injects a footer with a "Last updated" label, and includes the client-side script.

## How to run

```bash
# run all build steps
bash run.sh
```

`run.sh` is quiet by default: it prints each task name and the final result, while successful command output is hidden. For troubleshooting, run `VERBOSE=1 bash run.sh`.

## One page per section

Every `##` section of `sources/site.md` also gets its own address, named after the English heading:
`/publications.html`, `/services.html`, `/teaching.html`, `/news.html`, `/students.html`, `/public-talks.html`,
`/about-me.html`, and the same names under `/fr/`. Each page holds only its own section (so search engines see
distinct pages); its other tabs are links to the other pages. The home page keeps every section as tabs, and
clicking a tab there updates the address bar to match. The blog keeps its own archive at `/blog/`. A new `##`
section gets a page automatically.

All canonical, `hreflang`, sitemap and `robots.txt` addresses use the custom domain set as `SITE_URL` in
`build/build-site.py`.

## Short CV

`cv-short/` holds a one-page CV with the most important items, in English and French (`.md` and `.pdf`). It is
generated from `sources/cv.md` on every build and must not be edited by hand; what is kept from each section is
set by `RULES` in `build/short_cv.py`. It is published at `/short-cv/` and `/fr/short-cv/`.

`cv-full/` holds the full CV the same way: `cv-en.pdf`, `cv-fr.pdf`, and a readable `cv-en.md` / `cv-fr.md` with the
publication list filled in. These `.md` files are generated copies; edit `sources/cv.md` instead. The published
addresses are unchanged (`/cv/cv-en.pdf`, `/short-cv/`), so existing links to the CV keep working.

## Monthly automation

`.github/workflows/monthly-update.yml` runs on the last day of each month (and on demand from the Actions tab).
It does what `./run.sh` does, then commits and pushes:

1. `build/sync-updates.py` reads the Google Scholar profile and the conf.researchr.org profile.
   - A new paper is added to `resources/publications.bib`. For a paper found on conf.researchr.org, the authors,
     track and schedule come from its details page.
   - A new committee role is added to the Services section of `sources/site.md` and `sources/cv.md`.
2. If something was added, `run.sh` runs: the French files, the news, the CVs, the short CV and the site are rebuilt.
3. The result is committed and pushed. The commit message lists what was added and anything that needs attention.

To preview what the sync would do without writing anything: `python build/sync-updates.py --dry-run`.

### Hand fixes are kept

The automation never revisits something it has already handled, so a manual fix followed by `./run.sh` stays:

- `resources/sync-state.json` records every Scholar paper, researchr item and news line already handled. A recorded
  item is not imported or announced again, even after its entry is edited or deleted. Delete an ID from this file to
  have it reconsidered.
- `sources/news.md` and `sources/news-fr.md` only grow from the top: existing lines are never rewritten, so they can
  be edited or removed freely.
- `sources/site-fr.md` and `sources/cv-fr.md`: a line is translated again only when its English line changes
  (`resources/translation-state.json` tracks this). French lines corrected in place are kept. Adding or removing
  whole lines in a French file breaks the alignment and causes one full re-translation.

## Blog posts

Write English posts as Markdown files in `sources/blog/`. Files whose name starts with `_` are ignored, so `sources/blog/_template.md` is safe to keep as a template. Published posts are listed on `/blog/` and `/fr/blog/` as a full archive with grid/list views, search, year filters, and topic filters.

A post can be as small as:

```markdown
---
title: "My Post Title"
date: "2026-08-20"
description: "A short summary for indexes and SEO."
tags:
  - AI
draft: false
---

Post content in Markdown.
```

The opening `---` must start at the first column of the file. This is intentional: malformed front matter should fail the build instead of publishing broken instructions as a public article.

Then run `bash run.sh`. The build translates the post to French, writes the generated translation under `sources/blog-fr/`, publishes English and French HTML pages under `docs/blog/` and `docs/fr/blog/`, updates RSS feeds, and adds sitemap entries. Each post ends with an issue link for reporting problems.

Or run individual build targets:

```bash
bash runner/build-site.sh
bash runner/build-cv.sh
bash runner/build-industry.sh
```

## Deployment model

`docs/` is the publishable site directory for **GitHub Pages**.

So the normal edit cycle is:
1. Update content in `sources/` (and optionally templates/resources)
2. Rebuild
3. Commit generated output (`docs/`, `cv-full/`, `cv-short/`) and source changes
4. Push to GitHub

---

If you just want to update text/content, start with `sources/site.md` and rebuild.
